00:11:01,033 --> 00:11:02,950
It uses Monte Carlo tree
search with self-play

252
00:11:02,950 --> 00:11:05,720
to train a network that predicts
values and action probabilities.

253
00:11:05,720 --> 00:11:07,550
That's part of what it does.

254
00:11:07,550 --> 00:11:09,190
Well, wasn't there
a different thing

255
00:11:09,190 --> 00:11:13,690
that had the confidence for--

256
00:11:13,690 --> 00:11:14,750
Upper confidence trees?

257
00:11:14,750 --> 00:11:16,400
Yeah.

258
00:11:16,400 --> 00:11:19,350
Well, it uses a particular
form of upper confidence trees

259
00:11:19,350 --> 00:11:19,850
as well.

260
00:11:19,850 --> 00:11:22,790
So upper confidence trees is a
type of Monte Carlo tree search.

261
00:11:22,790 --> 00:11:25,455
It's like Monte Carlo tree
search is a superset of UCT.

262
00:11:37,700 --> 00:11:41,130
Let's go through these two,
because all of these are true.

263
00:11:41,130 --> 00:11:45,270
And so I think this is a useful
one to go through as well.

264
00:11:45,270 --> 00:11:50,970
So ChatGPT did learn
rewards from humans,

265
00:11:50,970 --> 00:11:53,060
preparing preferences
over prompt-output pairs.

266
00:11:53,060 --> 00:11:55,650
And then use PPO to
train a better prompt.

267
00:11:55,650 --> 00:12:00,260
So in fact, we did a ranking
where they did do this.

268
00:12:00,260 --> 00:12:04,700
In general, for long horizon
problems and really large

269
00:12:04,700 --> 00:12:07,272
action spaces, that
would be somewhere

270
00:12:07,272 --> 00:12:09,480
where forward search would
be really expensive to do.

271
00:12:09,480 --> 00:12:11,647
So using something like
AlphaZero, which essentially

272
00:12:11,647 --> 00:12:13,190
builds a subset of
the tree search,

273
00:12:13,190 --> 00:12:15,530
it can be really helpful.

274
00:12:15,530 --> 00:12:18,360
In PAC, probably
approximately correct methods.

275
00:12:18,360 --> 00:12:21,240
We are guaranteed to learn
an epsilon optimal policy.

276
00:12:21,240 --> 00:12:22,800
But the epsilon
might be non-zero,

277
00:12:22,800 --> 00:12:25,580
which means we're not guaranteed
to learn an optimal policy.

278
00:12:25,580 --> 00:12:28,670
So if you want to get a-- if
you're OK with your kitchen

279
00:12:28,670 --> 00:12:30,680
being slightly
messy, which I am,

280
00:12:30,680 --> 00:12:33,380
then it would be OK to
use a PAC RL algorithm.

281
00:12:33,380 --> 00:12:35,220
That would make a finite
number of mistakes.

282
00:12:35,220 --> 00:12:38,550
But most of the time, would
keep your kitchen pretty neat.

283
00:12:38,550 --> 00:12:40,740
But maybe not perfectly neat.

284
00:12:40,740 --> 00:12:42,630
And then offline RL
may be particularly

285
00:12:42,630 --> 00:12:45,060
beneficial for health care
and other high-stake settings

286
00:12:45,060 --> 00:12:50,013
where online exploration might
be risky or very expensive.

287
00:12:50,013 --> 00:12:51,680
So in this case, all
of these were true.

288
00:12:55,392 --> 00:12:57,350
And then the next one I
was going to talk about

289
00:12:57,350 --> 00:13:00,470
is 9, where we go through some
of the theoretical properties.

290
00:13:00,470 --> 00:13:09,260
So in this case, what we
have is that optimism--

291
00:13:09,260 --> 00:13:14,300
in this case, the
first one is not true,

292
00:13:14,300 --> 00:13:16,820
because we don't have
any guarantees in general

293
00:13:16,820 --> 00:13:19,100
for the REINFORCE algorithm.

294
00:13:19,100 --> 00:13:21,720
The second one is true for
the reasons we just said.

295
00:13:21,720 --> 00:13:24,660
A PAC algorithm guarantees
your epsilon optimal.

296
00:13:24,660 --> 00:13:28,460
So this is not
necessarily fully optimal.

297
00:13:28,460 --> 00:13:32,030
In the third case,
it is not guaranteed

298
00:13:32,030 --> 00:13:34,340
to have sublinear regret.

299
00:13:34,340 --> 00:13:35,940
So this is false.

300
00:13:35,940 --> 00:13:37,520
And the reason,
again, for this is

301
00:13:37,520 --> 00:13:39,780
that if you just get an
epsilon optimal policy,

302
00:13:39,780 --> 00:13:42,180
you might make epsilon
mistakes for the rest of time.

303
00:13:42,180 --> 00:13:46,610
So epsilon times t, which would
still give you a linear regret.

304
00:13:46,610 --> 00:13:48,330
The fourth is also false.

305
00:13:48,330 --> 00:13:50,758
So in general, you can think
of just minimizing regret.

306
00:13:50,758 --> 00:13:52,550
Which is the difference
between your policy

307
00:13:52,550 --> 00:13:53,660
and the optimal policy.

308
00:13:53,660 --> 00:13:56,237
Or maximizing expected
cumulative rewards.

309
00:13:56,237 --> 00:13:57,570
And they're just the same thing.

310
00:13:57,570 --> 00:13:59,520
One, you just subtract
from the other.

311
00:13:59,520 --> 00:14:01,350
Can either maximize
cumulative regret

312
00:14:01,350 --> 00:14:03,180
or minimize-- maximize
cumulative reward

313
00:14:03,180 --> 00:14:05,130
or minimize regret.

314
00:14:05,130 --> 00:14:14,010
And then in the fourth case,
this will not necessarily

315
00:14:14,010 --> 00:14:15,370
be a PAC algorithm.

316
00:14:15,370 --> 00:14:21,570
So the only one in this
case is only B is true.

317
00:14:21,570 --> 00:14:23,640
And the reason for
this is a PAC algorithm

318
00:14:23,640 --> 00:14:25,860
has to make a finite
number of mistakes.

319
00:14:25,860 --> 00:14:28,560
So it normally has
to be polynomial.

320
00:14:28,560 --> 00:14:31,170
And so even this would
say this algorithm

321
00:14:31,170 --> 00:14:33,510
would be consistently converging
to the optimal policy.

322
00:14:33,510 --> 00:14:35,760
But you don't necessarily
know how long it would take.

323
00:14:35,760 --> 00:14:38,490
So it could be very expensive.

324
00:14:38,490 --> 00:14:41,940
And then the final
question has us

325
00:14:41,940 --> 00:14:47,000
think about which algorithms
could generate the observed

326
00:14:47,000 --> 00:14:47,988
reward.

327
00:14:47,988 --> 00:14:50,280
Raise your hand if anybody
wants me to go through that?

328
00:14:50,280 --> 00:14:51,890
I'm happy to, and
step through it.

329
00:14:51,890 --> 00:14:54,720
Or otherwise, we'll move
on to the next slide.

330
00:14:54,720 --> 00:14:57,395
Allow the people to just
think about how different

331
00:14:57,395 --> 00:14:59,270
algorithms would run,
and whether or not they

332
00:14:59,270 --> 00:15:00,925
could generate
this observed data.

