00:35:33,410 --> 00:35:37,250
Let me just make this
big for a second.

620
00:35:37,250 --> 00:35:39,770
If you think of this, when
you do matrix multiplication,

621
00:35:39,770 --> 00:35:43,960
you have A1 times B1, and A2
plus A2 times B3, A1 times

622
00:35:43,960 --> 00:35:45,472
B2, A2 plus B4, et cetera.

623
00:35:45,472 --> 00:35:47,680
You can think of there's
all these different products

624
00:35:47,680 --> 00:35:48,400
and sums.

625
00:35:48,400 --> 00:35:50,170
And you could do them
in different orders.

626
00:35:50,170 --> 00:35:52,010
And you can kind
of refactor that.

627
00:35:52,010 --> 00:35:56,440
So when you think about all
the operations you could do,

628
00:35:56,440 --> 00:35:59,298
you're going to have to do a
series of operations such that--

629
00:35:59,298 --> 00:36:01,090
remember, what they're
trying to learn here

630
00:36:01,090 --> 00:36:03,410
is not how to multiply
two particular ones.

631
00:36:03,410 --> 00:36:05,620
But they're trying to
learn the algorithm that

632
00:36:05,620 --> 00:36:08,960
will always correctly solve in
the minimum number of steps.

633
00:36:08,960 --> 00:36:11,110
So here, their reward
function is the number

634
00:36:11,110 --> 00:36:13,300
of steps, the number
of computations

635
00:36:13,300 --> 00:36:14,510
that you have to do.

636
00:36:14,510 --> 00:36:16,190
And of course, it
has to be correct.

637
00:36:16,190 --> 00:36:18,230
And to me, one of the
brilliance of their ideas

638
00:36:18,230 --> 00:36:20,240
is, how do you make
sure that you're only

639
00:36:20,240 --> 00:36:23,260
