some form of offline phase.

749
00:41:03,510 --> 00:41:04,610
And that's exactly right.

750
00:41:04,610 --> 00:41:07,310
That's certainly what
they did in this case.

751
00:41:07,310 --> 00:41:11,240
I think it's interesting to
think about how it's represented

752
00:41:11,240 --> 00:41:13,032
and the different
types of controls

753
00:41:13,032 --> 00:41:14,990
you'd be applying in this
case, which generally

754
00:41:14,990 --> 00:41:16,577
will be real valued.

755
00:41:16,577 --> 00:41:18,660
So it's a very different
problem than AlphaTensor.

756
00:41:18,660 --> 00:41:21,500
Let's look at what
their architecture was.

757
00:41:21,500 --> 00:41:24,950
So in this case, one thing
that they also really emphasize

758
00:41:24,950 --> 00:41:27,283
in this is that they had to
spend quite a long time--

759
00:41:27,283 --> 00:41:28,700
they had to think
really carefully

760
00:41:28,700 --> 00:41:30,390
about what is the objective.

761
00:41:30,390 --> 00:41:31,770
So this is an interesting one.

762
00:41:31,770 --> 00:41:34,730
It's not just minimize
the number of computations

763
00:41:34,730 --> 00:41:36,450
to solve two matrices.

764
00:41:36,450 --> 00:41:39,800
It's saying, we want to be
able to manipulate plasma

765
00:41:39,800 --> 00:41:41,690
into particular configurations.

766
00:41:41,690 --> 00:41:43,510
And so you could
imagine, in this case,

767
00:41:43,510 --> 00:41:45,270
you might have lots of
different reward functions.

768
00:41:45,270 --> 00:41:46,937
And you want to be
able to quickly learn

769
00:41:46,937 --> 00:41:48,150
policies for those.

770
00:41:48,150 --> 00:41:53,100
So what they do to ameliorate
the offline safety issue

771
00:41:53,100 --> 00:41:54,690
is they build a simulator.

772
00:41:54,690 --> 00:41:57,690
And I was just coming
to someone that--

773
00:41:57,690 --> 00:41:59,520
on a recent panel I
was on, I was talking

774
00:41:59,520 --> 00:42:00,750
to a mechanical
engineer that said

775
00:42:00,750 --> 00:42:02,790
that's one of the reasons they
were really interested in AI

776
00:42:02,790 --> 00:42:05,040
and machine learning, is
they like to make simulators

777
00:42:05,040 --> 00:42:08,470
a really computationally
expensive physical processes.

778
00:42:08,470 --> 00:42:10,500
And so they here
have a simulator

779
00:42:10,500 --> 00:42:12,750
that is fairly high
fidelity, but not perfect.

780
00:42:12,750 --> 00:42:14,490
And is high fidelity
enough that they

781
00:42:14,490 --> 00:42:16,470
think it'll be useful,
but low fidelity enough

782
00:42:16,470 --> 00:42:18,390
that you could do
optimization over it.

783
00:42:18,390 --> 00:42:21,300
So what they're going to do in
this case is they are solving

784
00:42:21,300 --> 00:42:24,340
the offline case by
constructing here--

785
00:42:24,340 --> 00:42:27,450
not necessarily from data or
maybe from a physics model--

786
00:42:27,450 --> 00:42:28,330
a simulator.

787
00:42:28,330 --> 00:42:29,928
So we're going to
do model-based RL.

788
00:42:29,928 --> 00:42:31,470
Model-based in the
sense that we have

789
00:42:31,470 --> 00:42:33,480
to have a model or a simulator.

790
00:42:33,480 --> 00:42:36,340
But then what they're going to
do is an actor critic method.

791
00:42:36,340 --> 00:42:39,330
So they are going
to do actor critic,

792
00:42:39,330 --> 00:42:42,453
in this case, where they
have a control policy.

793
00:42:42,453 --> 00:42:43,870
And they also are
going to learn--

794
00:42:43,870 --> 00:42:45,130
so we have the actor here.

795
00:42:45,130 --> 00:42:47,940
And they're also going
to be learning a critic.

796
00:42:47,940 --> 00:42:51,690
So I thought this was pretty
interesting for why they took

797
00:42:51,690 --> 00:42:52,990
this particular architecture.

798
00:42:52,990 --> 00:42:55,323
I'm just going to read you a
little bit about that part.

799
00:42:55,323 --> 00:42:56,730
Let me go down there.

800
00:42:56,730 --> 00:42:58,420
So a couple of things.

801
00:42:58,420 --> 00:43:00,900
So I thought one of the things
is they use an actor critic

802
00:43:00,900 --> 00:43:03,100
method that is related
to something else we saw,

803
00:43:03,100 --> 00:43:04,240
but not exactly the same.

804
00:43:04,240 --> 00:43:05,200
It's called MPO.

805
00:43:05,200 --> 00:43:07,500
And I'll write that
out in a second.

806
00:43:07,500 --> 00:43:09,780
But one of the things that
I thought was interesting

807
00:43:09,780 --> 00:43:13,652
is they said, in our
simulating period,

808
00:43:13,652 --> 00:43:15,110
we can do sort of
whatever we want,

809
00:43:15,110 --> 00:43:17,690
and we can have a really
complicated critic when.

810
00:43:17,690 --> 00:43:20,660
We are deploying this,
it has to be real-time.

811
00:43:20,660 --> 00:43:22,910
So some other people, I think
it was-- brought this up

812
00:43:22,910 --> 00:43:24,480
when we were chatting about it.

813
00:43:24,480 --> 00:43:26,445
This is like self-driving cars.

814
00:43:26,445 --> 00:43:28,320
And you have to have
really fast controllers.

815
00:43:28,320 --> 00:43:30,080
You can't do Monte
Carlo tree search

816
00:43:30,080 --> 00:43:31,472
and wait for us to decide--

817
00:43:31,472 --> 00:43:33,180
like, the plasma's
going to do something.

818
00:43:33,180 --> 00:43:34,700
And so either you're
controlling it,

819
00:43:34,700 --> 00:43:36,830
or it is doing something
else if you're not

820
00:43:36,830 --> 00:43:38,190
making an active control.

821
00:43:38,190 --> 00:43:40,640
And so they needed an
actor, a.k.a. a policy,

822
00:43:40,640 --> 00:43:42,780
that is really
computationally fast.

823
00:43:42,780 --> 00:43:45,350
And so what they said is that,
inside of their actor critic

824
00:43:45,350 --> 00:43:46,892
architecture, one
of the reasons they

825
00:43:46,892 --> 00:43:48,800
wanted to do that
during the training

826
00:43:48,800 --> 00:43:50,870
is they could
require their actor

827
00:43:50,870 --> 00:43:52,670
to be pretty low-dimensional.

828
00:43:52,670 --> 00:43:54,110
And so have a
pretty small network

829
00:43:54,110 --> 00:43:57,120
to specify the actor
or the control policy,

830
00:43:57,120 --> 00:43:59,360
which is what they're
going to eventually deploy.

831
00:43:59,360 --> 00:44:01,980
But they could have a
really complicated critic.

832
00:44:01,980 --> 00:44:03,470
And so they can
leverage the fact

833
00:44:03,470 --> 00:44:06,000
that in the offline
setting, they can really,

834
00:44:06,000 --> 00:44:07,760
in a complicated
way, many parameters,

835
00:44:07,760 --> 00:44:09,230
specify their value function.

836
00:44:09,230 --> 00:44:10,970
Because this is all offline.

837
00:44:10,970 --> 00:44:13,190
And so this is of a nice,
interesting asymmetry

838
00:44:13,190 --> 00:44:16,110
between computational
efficiency,

839
00:44:16,110 --> 00:44:18,970
and what are the affordances you
have offline compared to online.

840
00:44:18,970 --> 00:44:20,970
So they have a very
complicated critic.

841
00:44:20,970 --> 00:44:22,900
And they have a
very simple actor.

842
00:44:22,900 --> 00:44:25,140
And so then they
train the actor to try

843
00:44:25,140 --> 00:44:27,870
to find a good point in that
policy space using their really

844
00:44:27,870 --> 00:44:30,150
complicated critic.

845
00:44:30,150 --> 00:44:31,808
And so they said,
the representation

846
00:44:31,808 --> 00:44:33,600
of the control policy
vector is restricted,

847
00:44:33,600 --> 00:44:36,670
as it must run on TBC
with real-time guarantees.

848
00:44:36,670 --> 00:44:38,410
But the critic is unrestricted.

849
00:44:38,410 --> 00:44:41,268
So I thought that was pretty
interesting that they had this.

850
00:44:41,268 --> 00:44:42,810
Now, another thing--
and this came up

851
00:44:42,810 --> 00:44:46,480
in some conversations--
is, as you might imagine,

852
00:44:46,480 --> 00:44:49,290
if we go from offline to online,
there is always the problem

853
00:44:49,290 --> 00:44:51,460
that it might not translate.

854
00:44:51,460 --> 00:44:53,020
And again, we're
dealing with plasma.

855
00:44:53,020 --> 00:44:55,180
So we want to have some
sort of safety guarantees.

856
00:44:55,180 --> 00:44:57,030
So here, the ideas
we've talked about

857
00:44:57,030 --> 00:45:02,070
before about having more trusted
regions or having pessimism

858
00:45:02,070 --> 00:45:02,735
come up.

859
00:45:02,735 --> 00:45:04,110
And the way that
they handle this

860
00:45:04,110 --> 00:45:06,510
is by putting it inside
of the reward function.

861
00:45:06,510 --> 00:45:08,820
So they essentially
define areas which they

862
00:45:08,820 --> 00:45:11,710
think could cause bad outcomes.

863
00:45:11,710 --> 00:45:13,710
And then they put that
inside their reward

864
00:45:13,710 --> 00:45:17,640
function to lead to a policy
that veers away from that area.

865
00:45:17,640 --> 00:45:20,490
And I think, again, that's
a pretty common idea

866
00:45:20,490 --> 00:45:21,760
that if you have safety--

867
00:45:21,760 --> 00:45:23,770
this comes up in robotics
and other ones, too.

868
00:45:23,770 --> 00:45:28,210
Claire Tomlin up at
Berkeley does this, too.

869
00:45:28,210 --> 00:45:29,050
A number of others.

870
00:45:29,050 --> 00:45:30,460
You put that inside of
the reward function,

871
00:45:30,460 --> 00:45:32,290
so the resulting
policy avoid those.

872
00:45:32,290 --> 00:45:35,850
And so here, they're doing that
not necessarily because reaching

873
00:45:35,850 --> 00:45:37,400
that particular
part would be bad,

874
00:45:37,400 --> 00:45:39,150
but because you're
getting close to a part

875
00:45:39,150 --> 00:45:40,650
where it might be
unsafe, or where

876
00:45:40,650 --> 00:45:44,070
you don't trust your simulator.

877
00:45:44,070 --> 00:45:45,550
So let's go back to here.

878
00:45:45,550 --> 00:45:49,360
So in this case,
it's an actor critic.

879
00:45:49,360 --> 00:45:51,850
Actor critic.

880
00:45:51,850 --> 00:45:53,366
This is complicated.

881
00:45:56,120 --> 00:45:56,980
This is simple.

882
00:46:00,710 --> 00:46:02,580
It has to be simple for speed.

883
00:46:02,580 --> 00:46:06,350
And we all do this
with a simulator.

884
00:46:06,350 --> 00:46:22,510
We put penalties and the
reward to avoid inaccuracies

885
00:46:22,510 --> 00:46:28,530
in simulator, or
unsafe outcomes.

886
00:46:31,370 --> 00:46:34,040
And so this is very similar to
this pessimism over the places

887
00:46:34,040 --> 00:46:36,710
where we're uncertain, whether
because of data sparsity,

888
00:46:36,710 --> 00:46:40,285
or because of known
problems in our simulator.

889
00:46:40,285 --> 00:46:42,290
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

890
00:46:49,997 --> 00:46:51,330
Or how do you double check that?

891
00:46:51,330 --> 00:46:54,427
I assume they really don't
want to be making [INAUDIBLE].

892
00:46:54,427 --> 00:46:55,260
So a great question.

893
00:46:55,260 --> 00:46:56,720
So my guess, in this
case, is that it ends up

894
00:46:56,720 --> 00:46:58,260
making you just
pretty conservative.

895
00:46:58,260 --> 00:47:02,540
I think of just how far away--
no, I assume in this case,

896
00:47:02,540 --> 00:47:04,790
maybe because some of the
physics simulators that they

897
00:47:04,790 --> 00:47:08,520
have access to, that they could
play with some of saying like,

898
00:47:08,520 --> 00:47:09,222
if you--

899
00:47:09,222 --> 00:47:11,180
how negative do you need
to make some of these?

900
00:47:11,180 --> 00:47:12,350
Or how out of bounds?

901
00:47:12,350 --> 00:47:14,210
Or how hard of a
constraint is that?

902
00:47:14,210 --> 00:47:16,640
So that you could be very
confident that before you

903
00:47:16,640 --> 00:47:19,028
deploy this, you make sure
that this doesn't reach there.

904
00:47:19,028 --> 00:47:21,320
At least in the simulator,
you could see whether or not

905
00:47:21,320 --> 00:47:22,778
you're violating
those constraints.

906
00:47:22,778 --> 00:47:24,710
Or if you have these
penalties, if you're

907
00:47:24,710 --> 00:47:26,750
sufficient not to
reach parts of the area

908
00:47:26,750 --> 00:47:29,000
that you think you
might want to avoid.

909
00:47:29,000 --> 00:47:31,620
Whether that will translate
to your real system

910
00:47:31,620 --> 00:47:33,450
is an important question.

911
00:47:33,450 --> 00:47:35,217
So, yeah, it's a great
issue of how you--

912
00:47:35,217 --> 00:47:37,800
no, I think it also introduces
the really interesting question

913
00:47:37,800 --> 00:47:39,535
of whether you can verify.

914
00:47:39,535 --> 00:47:40,660
So there are other methods.

915
00:47:40,660 --> 00:47:42,840
This is not some-- most of what
we've talked about is not those,

916
00:47:42,840 --> 00:47:44,280
but where you could
verify that you're not

917
00:47:44,280 --> 00:47:45,820
going to reach unsafe regions.

918
00:47:45,820 --> 00:47:49,860
And this would certainly be an
area you might want to do that.

919
00:47:49,860 --> 00:47:53,490
The third one was efficient and
targeted COVID-19 border testing

920
00:47:53,490 --> 00:47:54,110
for--

921
00:47:54,110 --> 00:47:58,700
I should have also mentioned--
so this is also a multi-step RL

922
00:47:58,700 --> 00:48:00,770
problem.

923
00:48:00,770 --> 00:48:02,893
So absolutely, the
controls you're doing

924
00:48:02,893 --> 00:48:03,810
affect the next state.

925
00:48:03,810 --> 00:48:04,680
And that's the whole point.

926
00:48:04,680 --> 00:48:06,170
And then you want to
manipulate the plasma

927
00:48:06,170 --> 00:48:07,380
into a particular occasion.

928
00:48:07,380 --> 00:48:09,070
So it's definitely
a multi-step system.

929
00:48:12,060 --> 00:48:14,610
This one is thinking
about how do you

930
00:48:14,610 --> 00:48:17,410
do efficient and targeted
COVID-19 border testing?

931
00:48:17,410 --> 00:48:19,260
And even though it's
via RL, it really

932
00:48:19,260 --> 00:48:21,430
is a bandit problem
in this case.

933
00:48:21,430 --> 00:48:25,440
So it's a repeated
bandit problem.

934
00:48:25,440 --> 00:48:30,890
It's a batch bandit
with delayed outcomes.

935
00:48:34,195 --> 00:48:35,820
So let's make this
a little bit bigger.

936
00:48:35,820 --> 00:48:38,910
So again, remember to think
back what happens in this case.

937
00:48:38,910 --> 00:48:40,555
People come in.

938
00:48:40,555 --> 00:48:42,680
Greece has some information
about those individuals

939
00:48:42,680 --> 00:48:43,920
before they show up.

940
00:48:43,920 --> 00:48:48,140
We have finite numbers of
tests we can run and process.

941
00:48:48,140 --> 00:48:50,683
We have a policy for each
individual coming off

942
00:48:50,683 --> 00:48:52,100
that plane, whether
or not they're

943
00:48:52,100 --> 00:48:55,700
going to be given no
test or they're tested.

944
00:48:55,700 --> 00:48:57,650
You get the results
24 hours later.

945
00:48:57,650 --> 00:48:59,750
And you use that to
update your policy.

946
00:48:59,750 --> 00:49:02,540
So I think this is a really nice
example of this batch bandit

947
00:49:02,540 --> 00:49:03,240
process.

948
00:49:03,240 --> 00:49:05,120
Who you test today
does not affect who

949
00:49:05,120 --> 00:49:06,540
arrives tomorrow on a plane.

950
00:49:06,540 --> 00:49:08,810
So it's a bandit problem.

951
00:49:08,810 --> 00:49:11,090
But we have this
delayed outcome problem

952
00:49:11,090 --> 00:49:13,760
that you don't observe the
outcomes of who you just

953
00:49:13,760 --> 00:49:14,910
tested for a while.

954
00:49:14,910 --> 00:49:17,420
Which means that algorithms
like Thompson sampling

955
00:49:17,420 --> 00:49:20,128
may be helpful.

956
00:49:20,128 --> 00:49:22,670
And then in addition, some of
the other really big challenges

957
00:49:22,670 --> 00:49:25,050
in this case is that you
have a lot of constraints.

958
00:49:28,700 --> 00:49:30,540
You have constraints
for multiple reasons.

959
00:49:30,540 --> 00:49:33,405
So we have constraints over
the number of tests we can run.

960
00:49:33,405 --> 00:49:35,030
You also can have
different constraints

961
00:49:35,030 --> 00:49:36,905
depending on where you're
arriving in Greece,

962
00:49:36,905 --> 00:49:38,280
and where you can send things.

963
00:49:38,280 --> 00:49:39,780
So there are different
testing sites

964
00:49:39,780 --> 00:49:41,550
which might have
different capacities.

965
00:49:41,550 --> 00:49:43,377
And in some cases,
also, you might have--

966
00:49:43,377 --> 00:49:45,210
I don't think they dealt
with in this paper,

967
00:49:45,210 --> 00:49:47,340
but sometimes you might have
fairness constraints, too.

968
00:49:47,340 --> 00:49:49,290
Like, maybe it's best
to test all the women,

969
00:49:49,290 --> 00:49:51,140
but maybe that's
considered unfair.

970
00:49:51,140 --> 00:49:54,200
And so you may have a number
of different constraints

971
00:49:54,200 --> 00:49:57,290
that you can think of as
restricting your policy class.

972
00:49:57,290 --> 00:50:01,230
So it's a pretty interesting
interaction problem here.

973
00:50:01,230 --> 00:50:03,690
And also because of the
fact that it's budgeted,

974
00:50:03,690 --> 00:50:06,140
it means that a lot of your
outcomes are coupled in a way

975
00:50:06,140 --> 00:50:07,670
that they might not be.

976
00:50:07,670 --> 00:50:10,033
So, for example, if
you give me a test--

977
00:50:10,033 --> 00:50:12,200
if we only have one test
that we can do in this room

978
00:50:12,200 --> 00:50:14,960
and you give me the test, then
you can't give it to any of you.

979
00:50:14,960 --> 00:50:19,730
And so there's this interaction,
too, in terms of the data

980
00:50:19,730 --> 00:50:22,535
that we get to
observe for the right.

981
00:50:22,535 --> 00:50:24,410
So I think this is a
really interesting case.

982
00:50:24,410 --> 00:50:26,285
And it is really
interesting that it ended up

983
00:50:26,285 --> 00:50:28,340
having a significant benefit.

984
00:50:28,340 --> 00:50:30,090
One of the things,
too, that's interesting

985
00:50:30,090 --> 00:50:32,323
about this is how we
define the reward.

986
00:50:32,323 --> 00:50:34,740
One thing that we were talking
about in our smaller groups

987
00:50:34,740 --> 00:50:37,680
is that, really, would like to
understand how this is impacting

988
00:50:37,680 --> 00:50:39,563
downstream COVID outcomes.

989
00:50:39,563 --> 00:50:40,980
And you can measure
those, but you

990
00:50:40,980 --> 00:50:42,160
can measure those really late.

991
00:50:42,160 --> 00:50:43,743
You can use those
as a way to evaluate

992
00:50:43,743 --> 00:50:45,850
how effective the
overall program was,

993
00:50:45,850 --> 00:50:48,640
but not necessarily a reward
you can use to optimize.

994
00:50:48,640 --> 00:50:50,473
And that's often a
really common challenge.

995
00:50:50,473 --> 00:50:52,140
The rewards you get
immediately that you

996
00:50:52,140 --> 00:50:54,090
could use to change
your policy may

997
00:50:54,090 --> 00:50:56,730
be different than the downstream
outcome you care about.

998
00:50:56,730 --> 00:50:59,910
And on Friday, I was at an
experimentation workshop

999
00:50:59,910 --> 00:51:02,520
at the business school here,
and I was giving a talk.

1000
00:51:02,520 --> 00:51:04,453
And I was really excited
and interested to see

1001
00:51:04,453 --> 00:51:06,120
how many other people
were also thinking

1002
00:51:06,120 --> 00:51:08,520
of this challenge of
short-term outcomes

1003
00:51:08,520 --> 00:51:11,580
versus long-term rewards
that you really care about.

1004
00:51:11,580 --> 00:51:13,950
And I think this comes
up a lot in advertising,

1005
00:51:13,950 --> 00:51:15,170
and other areas, too.

1006
00:51:15,170 --> 00:51:16,853
Companies like
Netflix, and Spotify,

1007
00:51:16,853 --> 00:51:19,020
and others we're talking
about this common challenge

1008
00:51:19,020 --> 00:51:21,900
where you have to make
policy decisions--

1009
00:51:21,900 --> 00:51:25,200
or update your policy
way before you can maybe

1010
00:51:25,200 --> 00:51:26,203
observe those outcomes.

1011
00:51:26,203 --> 00:51:28,120
And so if you have to
wait a really long time,

1012
00:51:28,120 --> 00:51:30,360
it limits how quickly
you can experiment.

1013
00:51:30,360 --> 00:51:32,520
And so in this case,
too, you might really

1014
00:51:32,520 --> 00:51:33,910
care about these
downstream ones.

1015
00:51:33,910 --> 00:51:35,368
But one of the
points of this paper

1016
00:51:35,368 --> 00:51:37,590
was to argue, looking
at that lagged

1017
00:51:37,590 --> 00:51:40,990
information was allowing people
to make not as good decisions.

1018
00:51:40,990 --> 00:51:44,123
And so you need these sort
of shorter term outcomes.

1019
00:51:44,123 --> 00:51:45,915
So do we have any
questions about this one?

1020
00:51:49,087 --> 00:51:50,670
So I encourage you
to-- if you haven't

1021
00:51:50,670 --> 00:51:52,290
read any of those
papers, they're

1022
00:51:52,290 --> 00:51:55,020
really beautiful papers, if you
want to read any of them or all.

1023
00:51:55,020 --> 00:51:57,353
And then just finally, if you
remember all the way back,

1024
00:51:57,353 --> 00:51:59,890
we talked about ChatGPT at the
very beginning of the class.

1025
00:51:59,890 --> 00:52:01,320
And I think you should
feel excited now

1026
00:52:01,320 --> 00:52:03,660
that you really understand
this whole pipeline of what's

1027
00:52:03,660 --> 00:52:04,480
possible.

1028
00:52:04,480 --> 00:52:07,170
The first is sort of training
a supervised policy, which

1029
00:52:07,170 --> 00:52:09,930
we could think of
as behavior cloning.

1030
00:52:09,930 --> 00:52:12,940
The second is doing direct
preference elicitation.

1031
00:52:12,940 --> 00:52:15,670
We did it with two pairs,
and then doing PPO.

1032
00:52:15,670 --> 00:52:17,720
And we also, of course,
did DPO as well.

1033
00:52:17,720 --> 00:52:20,220
So I think now, even though we
didn't do with large language

1034
00:52:20,220 --> 00:52:22,620
models, you really have a
sense of the whole process

1035
00:52:22,620 --> 00:52:25,320
you could use if you were to
train large language models

1036
00:52:25,320 --> 00:52:26,585
and do the fine tuning.

1037
00:52:29,570 --> 00:52:33,260
So now we're just going to wrap
up with some of the main ideas,

1038
00:52:33,260 --> 00:52:34,390
and then looking forward.

1039
00:52:34,390 --> 00:52:37,240
So if we think about the main
characteristics of reinforcement

1040
00:52:37,240 --> 00:52:39,910
learning, this idea of learning
directly from data to make

1041
00:52:39,910 --> 00:52:41,210
good decisions.

1042
00:52:41,210 --> 00:52:43,630
We've been thinking a lot
about optimization, delayed

1043
00:52:43,630 --> 00:52:46,460
consequences, exploration
and generalization.

1044
00:52:46,460 --> 00:52:48,272
And I think a key
thing just to remember,

1045
00:52:48,272 --> 00:52:50,480
if you didn't remember
anything else from this class,

1046
00:52:50,480 --> 00:52:52,630
is that one of the big
differences of reinforcement

1047
00:52:52,630 --> 00:52:55,690
learning is that, in general,
the actions impact the data

1048
00:52:55,690 --> 00:52:56,890
distribution.

1049
00:52:56,890 --> 00:52:59,150
Certainly, of the
rewards we observe.

1050
00:52:59,150 --> 00:53:01,550
But often, also of the
states we get to reach.

1051
00:53:01,550 --> 00:53:04,090
And that's just very, very
different than supervised

1052
00:53:04,090 --> 00:53:05,770
learning or
unsupervised learning,

1053
00:53:05,770 --> 00:53:09,040
where the data we get doesn't--

1054
00:53:09,040 --> 00:53:11,170
you always see the
label, or you just

1055
00:53:11,170 --> 00:53:15,100
have a static-generated
distribution of data.

1056
00:53:15,100 --> 00:53:18,430
So this is both a huge
opportunity and a huge challenge

1057
00:53:18,430 --> 00:53:21,225
because we have to think a lot
more about distribution shift.

1058
00:53:23,920 --> 00:53:25,780
So in terms of the
standard settings

1059
00:53:25,780 --> 00:53:28,570
we've seen, we've
talked about bandits,

1060
00:53:28,570 --> 00:53:30,820
where the next state is
independent of the prior state

1061
00:53:30,820 --> 00:53:31,420
and action.

1062
00:53:31,420 --> 00:53:33,640
As well as general
decision processes,

1063
00:53:33,640 --> 00:53:36,130
where the next
state might depend

1064
00:53:36,130 --> 00:53:38,870
on all the previous
actions and states.

1065
00:53:38,870 --> 00:53:40,563
Or it might be
Markov, and it only

1066
00:53:40,563 --> 00:53:41,980
depends on the
immediate state and

1067
00:53:41,980 --> 00:53:43,640
the immediate previous action.

1068
00:53:43,640 --> 00:53:47,320
We've also talked a lot about
the online/offline settings,

1069
00:53:47,320 --> 00:53:49,732
where either you have
historical data only

1070
00:53:49,732 --> 00:53:51,940
and you're trying to learn
better policies from that.

1071
00:53:51,940 --> 00:53:55,245
Or where you can actually
actively gather your own data.

1072
00:53:55,245 --> 00:53:56,620
And I will highlight
there that I

1073
00:53:56,620 --> 00:54:01,840
think many real-world settings
are often between these two.

1074
00:54:01,840 --> 00:54:02,340
Many.

1075
00:54:07,790 --> 00:54:10,410
So in many cases, you might have
a large pool of offline data,

1076
00:54:10,410 --> 00:54:12,410
and then you might be
able to get a small amount

1077
00:54:12,410 --> 00:54:13,730
of new online data.

1078
00:54:13,730 --> 00:54:14,927
This comes up in robotics.

1079
00:54:14,927 --> 00:54:16,260
It comes up in some of our work.

1080
00:54:16,260 --> 00:54:18,240
We often call this sort
of experimental design.

1081
00:54:18,240 --> 00:54:20,330
So that you might
have offline data,

1082
00:54:20,330 --> 00:54:21,920
and then you can
design an experiment

1083
00:54:21,920 --> 00:54:25,100
to gather a small amount of data
to try to learn a good decision

1084
00:54:25,100 --> 00:54:26,000
policy.

1085
00:54:26,000 --> 00:54:27,500
So I think, in
general, we can think

1086
00:54:27,500 --> 00:54:30,280
of this as an entire spectrum
between these two extremes.

1087
00:54:32,870 --> 00:54:34,950
Now, what are some of the
core ideas we've seen?

1088
00:54:34,950 --> 00:54:37,158
Well, of course, we've seen
a lot of different ideas.

1089
00:54:37,158 --> 00:54:38,960
But I think it's nice
to pop up a level

1090
00:54:38,960 --> 00:54:41,400
and think about
the common themes.

1091
00:54:41,400 --> 00:54:43,638
And Chelsea Finn,
who teaches Deep RL,

1092
00:54:43,638 --> 00:54:45,180
also had a really
nice slide on this.

1093
00:54:45,180 --> 00:54:47,990
So I found that my thoughts were
aligning with a number of hers

1094
00:54:47,990 --> 00:54:48,740
as well.

1095
00:54:48,740 --> 00:54:53,120
So one thing is just to be
really familiar with the fact

1096
00:54:53,120 --> 00:54:55,200
that when we have
function approximation--

1097
00:54:55,200 --> 00:54:57,492
which we're almost always
going to need because we want

1098
00:54:57,492 --> 00:54:58,980
to handle hard problems--

1099
00:54:58,980 --> 00:55:00,540
hard, complex problems.

1100
00:55:00,540 --> 00:55:02,600
And we want to do
off-policy learning

1101
00:55:02,600 --> 00:55:04,320
that, honestly, we
often want to do,

1102
00:55:04,320 --> 00:55:07,317
whether we're online or offline.

1103
00:55:07,317 --> 00:55:08,900
And just remember,
off-policy learning

1104
00:55:08,900 --> 00:55:11,400
just means that we want to take
some data that was generated

1105
00:55:11,400 --> 00:55:14,720
from one decision policy and
use it to think about how

1106
00:55:14,720 --> 00:55:16,200
another one might work.

1107
00:55:16,200 --> 00:55:17,660
Whether in terms
of gradient steps,

1108
00:55:17,660 --> 00:55:20,510
or in terms of fully
offline learning.

1109
00:55:20,510 --> 00:55:22,740
And this is generally
just really hard.

1110
00:55:22,740 --> 00:55:25,100
So you could argue
that a huge number

1111
00:55:25,100 --> 00:55:27,290
of papers in reinforcement
learning just

1112
00:55:27,290 --> 00:55:28,470
think about this problem.

1113
00:55:28,470 --> 00:55:29,760
It's just incredibly hard.

1114
00:55:29,760 --> 00:55:32,868
And the reason is that
whenever we have a new policy,

1115
00:55:32,868 --> 00:55:35,160
we're going to get a new
distribution over state action

1116
00:55:35,160 --> 00:55:36,300
rewards.

1117
00:55:36,300 --> 00:55:39,820
And that means that it may
not match our current data.

1118
00:55:39,820 --> 00:55:41,730
We have a data
distribution shift.

1119
00:55:41,730 --> 00:55:44,320
And the reason we
want to do this--

1120
00:55:44,320 --> 00:55:46,560
the reason we want to
have use the offline data

1121
00:55:46,560 --> 00:55:49,100
is because we want
to be data efficient.

1122
00:55:49,100 --> 00:55:51,930
And this is true even
if you can be online.

1123
00:55:51,930 --> 00:55:54,930
Because as we saw
for things like PPO,

1124
00:55:54,930 --> 00:55:57,840
if you follow the theory,
or if you follow this,

1125
00:55:57,840 --> 00:56:00,860
you often have to really
be incredibly conservative,

1126
00:56:00,860 --> 00:56:04,010
or just have bad performance
for a very long time.

1127
00:56:04,010 --> 00:56:07,830
But the problem is that when we
combine these two, in general,

1128
00:56:07,830 --> 00:56:10,670
we're going to be doing
generalization or extrapolation.

1129
00:56:10,670 --> 00:56:13,920
And whenever we do that,
we need to be worried that,

1130
00:56:13,920 --> 00:56:16,040
like the values that
our predictions of how

1131
00:56:16,040 --> 00:56:19,760
good a policy will be, will not
match its actual performance.

1132
00:56:19,760 --> 00:56:22,080
And so over and
over and over again,

1133
00:56:22,080 --> 00:56:24,770
we've seen, how do we
try to mitigate this

1134
00:56:24,770 --> 00:56:26,270
in different types of methods?

1135
00:56:26,270 --> 00:56:29,900
So in PPO, the way we
control this-- and this is

1136
00:56:29,900 --> 00:56:32,100
an online method-- is we
control it with clipping.

1137
00:56:32,100 --> 00:56:36,110
We just can't take too big of
a step inside of our gradients.

1138
00:56:36,110 --> 00:56:39,020
And that allows us to make
sure that we are limiting

1139
00:56:39,020 --> 00:56:40,820
this extrapolation problem.

1140
00:56:40,820 --> 00:56:43,160
In the DAGGER case,
we mitigated this

1141
00:56:43,160 --> 00:56:44,850
by getting more expert labels.

1142
00:56:44,850 --> 00:56:47,720
We knew that there could
be a data distribution

1143
00:56:47,720 --> 00:56:51,100
shift when we started to follow
our behavior clone policy.

1144
00:56:51,100 --> 00:56:52,980
And so we just try
to get more labels

1145
00:56:52,980 --> 00:56:55,170
when we get into states
where we make decisions

1146
00:56:55,170 --> 00:56:56,470
different than the expert.

1147
00:56:56,470 --> 00:56:59,460
So we can cover the
distribution of states we

1148
00:56:59,460 --> 00:57:01,950
reach under the learned policy.

1149
00:57:01,950 --> 00:57:04,090
And things like
pessimistic Q Learning,

1150
00:57:04,090 --> 00:57:05,760
which came from my lab.

1151
00:57:05,760 --> 00:57:07,037
CQL, which came from Berkeley.

1152
00:57:07,037 --> 00:57:09,120
And MOPO, which came from
other colleagues of mine

1153
00:57:09,120 --> 00:57:12,750
here at Stanford, all introduce
pessimism into offline RL.

1154
00:57:12,750 --> 00:57:16,590
Again, exactly to limit
this extrapolation problem

1155
00:57:16,590 --> 00:57:20,105
where you're overly optimistic
about what will happen.

1156
00:57:20,105 --> 00:57:21,480
So I don't think
you should think

1157
00:57:21,480 --> 00:57:24,490
of these as being the only
ways to solve this problem.

1158
00:57:24,490 --> 00:57:27,247
I think what they should
inspire you to is to think, wow,

1159
00:57:27,247 --> 00:57:28,830
this is a problem
that comes up really

1160
00:57:28,830 --> 00:57:30,820
throughout all of
reinforcement learning.

1161
00:57:30,820 --> 00:57:33,118
And we have some methods
for trying to handle this.

1162
00:57:33,118 --> 00:57:34,910
But this is certainly
not a solved problem.

1163
00:57:38,020 --> 00:57:39,610
Some of the other
core ideas that we

1164
00:57:39,610 --> 00:57:41,170
saw a lot was this
idea of there's

1165
00:57:41,170 --> 00:57:43,870
different ways we could
think about the main objects

1166
00:57:43,870 --> 00:57:45,360
in reinforcement learning.

1167
00:57:45,360 --> 00:57:48,138
So we had this sort of
models, values, and policies.

1168
00:57:48,138 --> 00:57:50,680
Sometimes people ask me like,
do we really need all of these?

1169
00:57:50,680 --> 00:57:52,750
Or are these all useful ideas?

1170
00:57:52,750 --> 00:57:55,480
I think some of the application
areas we were just going through

1171
00:57:55,480 --> 00:57:58,130
illustrate why these
might all be useful ideas.

1172
00:57:58,130 --> 00:58:03,340
So models are often easier
ways to represent uncertainty.

1173
00:58:03,340 --> 00:58:05,380
So if we only have
finite data and we're

1174
00:58:05,380 --> 00:58:08,750
training something about a
value, or a model, or a policy,

1175
00:58:08,750 --> 00:58:10,240
often, it might
be easiest for us

1176
00:58:10,240 --> 00:58:13,210
to represent that
uncertainty with a model.

1177
00:58:13,210 --> 00:58:15,290
So we have an idea
of why that might be.

1178
00:58:15,290 --> 00:58:16,810
Why might it be
easier to represent

1179
00:58:16,810 --> 00:58:20,100
uncertainty for a model rather
than a Q function or a policy?

1180
00:58:23,480 --> 00:58:24,990
You could disagree with me, too.

1181
00:58:24,990 --> 00:58:27,955
But I can give you why I think
this might be the easiest.

1182
00:58:32,502 --> 00:58:34,710
We're building just like a
dynamics model or a reward

1183
00:58:34,710 --> 00:58:35,880
model.

1184
00:58:35,880 --> 00:58:37,380
Why wouldn't that
be an easier place

1185
00:58:37,380 --> 00:58:41,240
for us to represent
our uncertainty about?

1186
00:58:41,240 --> 00:58:43,370
How the world works
compared to trying

1187
00:58:43,370 --> 00:58:45,145
to represent our
uncertainty over the Q

1188
00:58:45,145 --> 00:58:46,195
function or the policy.

1189
00:58:50,890 --> 00:58:55,850
Isn't it just because when
you're making-- when you're just

1190
00:58:55,850 --> 00:58:58,520
like [INAUDIBLE] uncertainty in
your policy, there's uncertainty

1191
00:58:58,520 --> 00:59:00,700
both [INAUDIBLE], but
also uncertainty about

1192
00:59:00,700 --> 00:59:02,450
given your assumption
of the world of what

1193
00:59:02,450 --> 00:59:04,010
you think the best action is.

1194
00:59:04,010 --> 00:59:06,120
So you're just dealing
with a joint uncertainty,

1195
00:59:06,120 --> 00:59:08,510
whereas the model of the
world is kind of like--

1196
00:59:08,510 --> 00:59:11,955
it's a more specified problem,
like one source of uncertainty.

1197
00:59:11,955 --> 00:59:13,080
Yeah, I think that's great.

1198
00:59:13,080 --> 00:59:14,122
That's a great intuition.

1199
00:59:14,122 --> 00:59:15,870
That's what I was
going for here.

1200
00:59:15,870 --> 00:59:19,250
So to repeat what I said here,
when you think about policy

1201
00:59:19,250 --> 00:59:20,885
uncertainty, there's--

1202
00:59:20,885 --> 00:59:22,537
that kind of
combines and wraps up

1203
00:59:22,537 --> 00:59:24,620
this idea of uncertainty
over how the world works,

1204
00:59:24,620 --> 00:59:26,390
and uncertainty
over what you should

1205
00:59:26,390 --> 00:59:29,180
do to make good decisions
given that world.

1206
00:59:29,180 --> 00:59:31,035
And same for the Q function.

1207
00:59:31,035 --> 00:59:33,410
And there are ways to directly
represent your uncertainty

1208
00:59:33,410 --> 00:59:35,430
over the policies
and the Q functions.

1209
00:59:35,430 --> 00:59:38,520
But models, it's a
prediction problem.

1210
00:59:38,520 --> 00:59:41,330
And so we have lots of tools
from supervised learning,

1211
00:59:41,330 --> 00:59:43,340
and from statistics
and data science

1212
00:59:43,340 --> 00:59:45,110
to think about modeling
our uncertainty

1213
00:59:45,110 --> 00:59:47,010
when it's just a
prediction problem.

1214
00:59:47,010 --> 00:59:49,400
Like, what state
will happen next?

1215
00:59:49,400 --> 00:59:51,420
Or what reward will
I get in this state?

1216
00:59:51,420 --> 00:59:53,860
There's no planning or
decision making yet.

1217
00:59:53,860 --> 00:59:55,060
It's just prediction.

1218
00:59:55,060 --> 00:59:56,820
And so it's sort of
a nice place for us

1219
00:59:56,820 --> 00:59:59,430
to reduce or leverage the
beautiful history of work

1220
00:59:59,430 --> 01:00:02,730
in all the other fields of
how we can do this easily,

1221
01:00:02,730 --> 01:00:06,570
instead of then having to
propagate that through.

1222
01:00:06,570 --> 01:00:08,580
So I think, often,
this is an easier place

1223
01:00:08,580 --> 01:00:10,060
to represent our uncertainty.

1224
01:00:10,060 --> 01:00:11,688
Of course, there's
no free lunch.

1225
01:00:11,688 --> 01:00:13,230
If we have it there,
we want to think

1226
01:00:13,230 --> 01:00:16,630
about our uncertainty over
policies and value functions,

1227
01:00:16,630 --> 01:00:17,890
we still have to propagate it.

1228
01:00:17,890 --> 01:00:20,190
But it may be easier for us
to represent that and drive

1229
01:00:20,190 --> 01:00:21,302
ourselves towards it.

1230
01:00:21,302 --> 01:00:23,760
They're also really useful for
things like Monte Carlo tree

1231
01:00:23,760 --> 01:00:24,510
search.

1232
01:00:24,510 --> 01:00:28,320
You can use models for
simulators, or for plasma.

1233
01:00:28,320 --> 01:00:30,510
You may be able to use
these ones as a place

1234
01:00:30,510 --> 01:00:35,760
to think about risky domains,
or to be very data efficient.

1235
01:00:35,760 --> 01:00:39,810
The Q function, in some ways, is
kind of the central part of RL.

1236
01:00:39,810 --> 01:00:41,310
In the sense that
it just summarizes

1237
01:00:41,310 --> 01:00:43,050
the performance of your policy.

1238
01:00:43,050 --> 01:00:45,660
And you can use it
often to directly act,

1239
01:00:45,660 --> 01:00:48,430
because you just take an argmax
with respect to the Q function.

1240
01:00:48,430 --> 01:00:52,470
So it's a good way to
summarize how good things are.

1241
01:00:52,470 --> 01:00:56,520
And policies are just
ultimately what we want to have.

1242
01:00:56,520 --> 01:00:58,830
We want to have good
decision making.

1243
01:00:58,830 --> 01:01:01,090
We often want to know
exactly how good that is.

1244
01:01:01,090 --> 01:01:02,700
And that's maybe
where the Q function

1245
01:01:02,700 --> 01:01:04,210
is one particular nice thing.

1246
01:01:04,210 --> 01:01:05,793
But ultimately, we
want to try to make

1247
01:01:05,793 --> 01:01:07,762
good decisions in the world.

1248
01:01:07,762 --> 01:01:09,720
I think another thing
that's come up repeatedly

1249
01:01:09,720 --> 01:01:14,155
is this question of computation
versus data efficiency.

1250
01:01:14,155 --> 01:01:16,530
And I think one thing that
it's really useful to remember

1251
01:01:16,530 --> 01:01:19,050
is that in some cases,
they are the same.

1252
01:01:19,050 --> 01:01:22,110
So in this class, I've
often talked as if they're

1253
01:01:22,110 --> 01:01:23,620
sort of totally different.

1254
01:01:23,620 --> 01:01:27,730
But in many situations,
if you have a simulator,

1255
01:01:27,730 --> 01:01:30,340
data is the same as computation.

1256
01:01:30,340 --> 01:01:32,250
You're either using
your computation

1257
01:01:32,250 --> 01:01:34,980
to maybe do more planning, or
try to get to a better policy

1258
01:01:34,980 --> 01:01:36,790
before you simulate
the next step.

1259
01:01:36,790 --> 01:01:39,180
Or you're just
simulating more steps.

1260
01:01:39,180 --> 01:01:41,950
And so I think when
you look at papers,

1261
01:01:41,950 --> 01:01:44,880
if they have a simulated
domain and they're trying to do

1262
01:01:44,880 --> 01:01:46,630
something really
fancy in the back,

1263
01:01:46,630 --> 01:01:49,200
it's useful to remind yourself
that if it was a real problem

1264
01:01:49,200 --> 01:01:51,390
you want to solve, you
could either take that same

1265
01:01:51,390 --> 01:01:54,450
computation and just have
maybe 10x more samples.

1266
01:01:54,450 --> 01:01:58,530
Or you can do 10x more
computation between each sample.

1267
01:01:58,530 --> 01:02:03,960
Now, in some other cases, we
really do have limited data.

1268
01:02:03,960 --> 01:02:09,190
We just fortunately do not have
7 billion people with COVID.

1269
01:02:09,190 --> 01:02:11,350
There's just a finite
number of people.

1270
01:02:11,350 --> 01:02:13,060
And there's a finite
number of students.

1271
01:02:13,060 --> 01:02:15,690
And so sometimes you really
want to be data efficient.

1272
01:02:15,690 --> 01:02:18,210
When you do that,
it's often trading off

1273
01:02:18,210 --> 01:02:19,770
for computational cost.

1274
01:02:19,770 --> 01:02:21,600
So we're going to try
to squeeze everything

1275
01:02:21,600 --> 01:02:22,750
we can out of the data.

1276
01:02:22,750 --> 01:02:23,850
And when we do
that, we often are

1277
01:02:23,850 --> 01:02:26,308
going to rely on methods that
are much more computationally

1278
01:02:26,308 --> 01:02:27,450
intensive.

1279
01:02:27,450 --> 01:02:29,830
And also, as you've
seen in some cases,

1280
01:02:29,830 --> 01:02:31,510
you have real
constraints on this.

1281
01:02:31,510 --> 01:02:32,560
Like in plasma.

1282
01:02:32,560 --> 01:02:34,390
Like in self-driving cars.

1283
01:02:34,390 --> 01:02:35,292
Like in robotics.

1284
01:02:35,292 --> 01:02:36,750
There are sometimes
cases where you

1285
01:02:36,750 --> 01:02:41,370
have to have fast computation,
because otherwise, there is

1286
01:02:41,370 --> 01:02:43,193
a default. There's kind
of a hidden action,

1287
01:02:43,193 --> 01:02:45,610
which is, you have to make a
decision at every time point.

1288
01:02:45,610 --> 01:02:47,200
If you're not doing
something optimal,

1289
01:02:47,200 --> 01:02:48,367
something else is happening.

1290
01:02:48,367 --> 01:02:50,590
There's some default action
that's always occurring.

1291
01:02:53,490 --> 01:02:55,240
Now, what are some of
the open challenges?

1292
01:02:55,240 --> 01:02:57,230
I think there's a lot
of open challenges.

1293
01:02:57,230 --> 01:03:00,950
I think RL is a
fascinating area.

1294
01:03:00,950 --> 01:03:04,780
But RL has not yet had
the applicational impact

1295
01:03:04,780 --> 01:03:09,430
that we've seen in some other
areas of AI and engineering.

1296
01:03:09,430 --> 01:03:12,490
And I think this is for
a number of reasons.

1297
01:03:12,490 --> 01:03:14,290
But one of them
is that you really

1298
01:03:14,290 --> 01:03:17,200
want methods that are
off-the-shelf and robust and

1299
01:03:17,200 --> 01:03:18,460
reliable.

1300
01:03:18,460 --> 01:03:22,120
And many RL algorithms
have hyperparameters.

1301
01:03:22,120 --> 01:03:23,770
You have to pick
the learning rate.

1302
01:03:23,770 --> 01:03:25,270
You have to pick--
some of these are

1303
01:03:25,270 --> 01:03:27,910
the same as normal machine
learning, and others of them

1304
01:03:27,910 --> 01:03:28,515
are different.

1305
01:03:28,515 --> 01:03:29,890
And one of the
challenges here is

1306
01:03:29,890 --> 01:03:33,235
if you're online, even though
in our world, like when we're

1307
01:03:33,235 --> 01:03:35,110
doing a homework, you
might be able to try it

1308
01:03:35,110 --> 01:03:36,402
with different hyperparameters.

1309
01:03:36,402 --> 01:03:39,010
In a real-world setting,
like for health care

1310
01:03:39,010 --> 01:03:42,320
or for customers, you would
just have that one trajectory.

1311
01:03:42,320 --> 01:03:44,660
And so in that case,
or one deployment,

1312
01:03:44,660 --> 01:03:47,190
you can't optimize
those parameters.

1313
01:03:47,190 --> 01:03:48,940
And so I think that
there's this real need

1314
01:03:48,940 --> 01:03:52,300
for automatic hyperparameter
tuning, model selection.

1315
01:03:52,300 --> 01:03:54,700
By that I mean, how do you
figure out what architecture

1316
01:03:54,700 --> 01:03:55,200
you use.

1317
01:03:55,200 --> 01:03:58,070
How do you even write
down the problem?

1318
01:03:58,070 --> 01:04:01,310
And generally robust methods,
model selection terms

1319
01:04:01,310 --> 01:04:03,780
like the size of your
neural network, et cetera.

1320
01:04:03,780 --> 01:04:05,930
And just general sort
of robust guarantees

1321
01:04:05,930 --> 01:04:07,700
that we're not going
to suddenly have one

1322
01:04:07,700 --> 01:04:11,870
run where your
performance is really bad.

1323
01:04:11,870 --> 01:04:13,760
The other is that
we often need things

1324
01:04:13,760 --> 01:04:15,560
that are going to
be able to span

1325
01:04:15,560 --> 01:04:18,690
this data versus
computation efficiency.

1326
01:04:18,690 --> 01:04:20,750
And we don't normally
have very good ways

1327
01:04:20,750 --> 01:04:23,180
to allow a practitioner to say
like, OK, well, this is how

1328
01:04:23,180 --> 01:04:24,800
much I care about this or that.

1329
01:04:24,800 --> 01:04:26,300
It'd be really nice
if we could have

1330
01:04:26,300 --> 01:04:27,740
sort of like Pareto frontiers.

1331
01:04:27,740 --> 01:04:33,060
And you could say, well, if this
is computation and this is data,

1332
01:04:33,060 --> 01:04:35,300
you might say, OK, I
want to have things

1333
01:04:35,300 --> 01:04:37,370
that are always somehow
optimally trading

1334
01:04:37,370 --> 01:04:38,628
off between those two.

1335
01:04:38,628 --> 01:04:40,170
And depending on my
application area,

1336
01:04:40,170 --> 01:04:42,770
I can pick where I want
to be on this curve.

1337
01:04:42,770 --> 01:04:45,140
And I also think this
hybrid offline-online case

1338
01:04:45,140 --> 01:04:46,430
is a really important one.

1339
01:04:46,430 --> 01:04:48,110
Where many
organizations might be

1340
01:04:48,110 --> 01:04:50,940
willing to do a little bit of
additional data collection,

1341
01:04:50,940 --> 01:04:53,720
but not fully online learning.

1342
01:04:53,720 --> 01:04:55,900
I think there's also some
just really big questions

1343
01:04:55,900 --> 01:04:57,320
for reinforcement learning.

1344
01:04:57,320 --> 01:05:00,590
We focused a lot on the Markov
decision process formulation.

1345
01:05:00,590 --> 01:05:02,840
That's where it comes
out of the 1950s.

1346
01:05:02,840 --> 01:05:04,992
And Bellman, that's
how I learned about it,

1347
01:05:04,992 --> 01:05:05,950
many people learned it.

1348
01:05:05,950 --> 01:05:08,840
And it has some really nice
intellectual properties.

1349
01:05:08,840 --> 01:05:11,170
But it is not clear that
this is the right way

1350
01:05:11,170 --> 01:05:13,480
to solve data-driven
decision making.

1351
01:05:13,480 --> 01:05:15,460
This is one framework.

1352
01:05:15,460 --> 01:05:20,050
So I had a professor when
I was a grad student, who

1353
01:05:20,050 --> 01:05:24,623
said that the whole world
is a multi-agent partially

1354
01:05:24,623 --> 01:05:26,290
observable Markov
decision process where

1355
01:05:26,290 --> 01:05:27,350
you're doing learning.

1356
01:05:27,350 --> 01:05:29,860
But it doesn't mean you
want to solve it like that.

1357
01:05:29,860 --> 01:05:31,900
And so while, in
many times, we might

1358
01:05:31,900 --> 01:05:35,680
be able to model things in
these kind of stochastic Markov

1359
01:05:35,680 --> 01:05:38,155
decision process ways,
that may or may not

1360
01:05:38,155 --> 01:05:40,280
be the most efficient way
to represent the problem.

1361
01:05:40,280 --> 01:05:41,738
It's just like how
you could always

1362
01:05:41,738 --> 01:05:44,510
represent a bandit as a
really complicated RL problem.

1363
01:05:44,510 --> 01:05:47,350
But if your next states are
independent of your previous

1364
01:05:47,350 --> 01:05:48,970
one, why would you do that?

1365
01:05:48,970 --> 01:05:51,140
So I think there's some
real questions over like,

1366
01:05:51,140 --> 01:05:54,168
are there better formulations?

1367
01:05:54,168 --> 01:05:56,710
I think a second thing is that,
historically in reinforcement

1368
01:05:56,710 --> 01:05:58,835
learning-- and even throughout
most of this class--

1369
01:05:58,835 --> 01:06:01,480
we focused on, I'm going
to learn from this one task

1370
01:06:01,480 --> 01:06:03,040
from scratch.

1371
01:06:03,040 --> 01:06:04,910
But of course, that's
not what humans do.

1372
01:06:04,910 --> 01:06:07,040
We constantly are building
on our prior experience.

1373
01:06:07,040 --> 01:06:08,920
We are sort of imperfect
agents for learning

1374
01:06:08,920 --> 01:06:10,820
across many, many, many tasks.

1375
01:06:10,820 --> 01:06:13,630
And what we've seen
from generative AI--

1376
01:06:13,630 --> 01:06:16,460
sort of large language
models, et cetera,

1377
01:06:16,460 --> 01:06:19,990
is that doing many, many tasks
might be really powerful.

1378
01:06:19,990 --> 01:06:22,840
And that's been relatively
understudied in the RL setting.

1379
01:06:22,840 --> 01:06:25,070
And it might be
much more effective.

1380
01:06:25,070 --> 01:06:28,195
We've seen even in like
AlphaZero, and AlphaTensor,

1381
01:06:28,195 --> 01:06:30,070
and others, that these
shared representations

1382
01:06:30,070 --> 01:06:31,717
can have huge benefits.

1383
01:06:31,717 --> 01:06:33,550
And so those might be
really productive ways

1384
01:06:33,550 --> 01:06:36,280
to think about accelerating
the speed of decision

1385
01:06:36,280 --> 01:06:39,760
making and learning good
data driven policies.

1386
01:06:39,760 --> 01:06:42,490
I think a third thing is
thinking about alternative forms

1387
01:06:42,490 --> 01:06:43,930
of feedback.

1388
01:06:43,930 --> 01:06:46,570
Assuming you get
single scalar rewards,

1389
01:06:46,570 --> 01:06:48,580
it's pretty limiting,
particularly

1390
01:06:48,580 --> 01:06:50,210
now that we have
large language models.

1391
01:06:50,210 --> 01:06:52,570
You could imagine having
really rich feedback

1392
01:06:52,570 --> 01:06:53,830
or really sparse feedback.

1393
01:06:53,830 --> 01:06:56,270
Like, thumbs up, thumbs
down, or preference pairs.

1394
01:06:56,270 --> 01:06:59,360
Or really detailed examples
about how something is wrong

1395
01:06:59,360 --> 01:07:01,040
or what your preferences are.

1396
01:07:01,040 --> 01:07:04,770
And now that we can start
to have language as rewards,

1397
01:07:04,770 --> 01:07:06,860
I think that's a much
richer opportunity.

1398
01:07:06,860 --> 01:07:10,250
And people are starting
to explore this already.

1399
01:07:10,250 --> 01:07:12,960
Another sort of just
what settings we're in.

1400
01:07:12,960 --> 01:07:16,370
Most of this class, we thought
about stochastic settings.

1401
01:07:16,370 --> 01:07:18,660
Take an action from a state.

1402
01:07:18,660 --> 01:07:21,530
You get to some next state
generated sort of stochastically

1403
01:07:21,530 --> 01:07:26,340
from some indifferent process.

1404
01:07:26,340 --> 01:07:28,603
But that's not very common
in real-world settings.

1405
01:07:28,603 --> 01:07:30,020
In many real-world
settings, there

1406
01:07:30,020 --> 01:07:32,660
are other stakeholders
or multi agents

1407
01:07:32,660 --> 01:07:35,595
that might be adversarial,
or might be cooperative.

1408
01:07:35,595 --> 01:07:37,220
You might have a
teacher that's helping

1409
01:07:37,220 --> 01:07:38,550
the agent learn something.

1410
01:07:38,550 --> 01:07:40,092
Or you might have
an adversary that's

1411
01:07:40,092 --> 01:07:41,310
competing with that agent.

1412
01:07:41,310 --> 01:07:45,230
And so those settings are also
really important to consider.

1413
01:07:45,230 --> 01:07:48,783
And I think another question,
too, is, throughout this class,

1414
01:07:48,783 --> 01:07:51,200
we've been thinking about
integrating, and doing learning,

1415
01:07:51,200 --> 01:07:53,617
and planning, and decision
making all at once, everything.

1416
01:07:53,617 --> 01:07:55,810
And that's wonderful
and elegant.

1417
01:07:55,810 --> 01:07:58,280
But there are many
approximations to this.

1418
01:07:58,280 --> 01:08:01,690
So in some other fields, they
often do system identification.

1419
01:08:01,690 --> 01:08:04,310
Like you might learn how the
Markov decision process works.

1420
01:08:04,310 --> 01:08:06,700
You learn your dynamics model,
you learn your word model.

1421
01:08:06,700 --> 01:08:09,100
You stop, you plant.

1422
01:08:09,100 --> 01:08:12,290
And so while this
offers some flexibility,

1423
01:08:12,290 --> 01:08:14,860
it also introduces
a lot of complexity.

1424
01:08:14,860 --> 01:08:16,510
And again, in some
areas, there might

1425
01:08:16,510 --> 01:08:19,115
be some really good
alternatives to this.

1426
01:08:19,115 --> 01:08:20,740
And finally, this is
one that's perhaps

1427
01:08:20,740 --> 01:08:22,323
closest to my heart,
which is, I think

1428
01:08:22,323 --> 01:08:23,830
that there's just
an enormous room

1429
01:08:23,830 --> 01:08:27,910
to do better data-driven
decision making

1430
01:08:27,910 --> 01:08:29,660
in domains that could benefit.

1431
01:08:29,660 --> 01:08:31,793
So I think there are
lots of application areas

1432
01:08:31,793 --> 01:08:32,960
we've talked about in class.

1433
01:08:32,960 --> 01:08:34,569
But there's so
many areas where I

1434
01:08:34,569 --> 01:08:37,399
think our society could benefit
from better decision making.

1435
01:08:37,399 --> 01:08:39,736
And so it'd be incredible
to see more of that impact,

1436
01:08:39,736 --> 01:08:41,319
whether it's from
the frameworks we've

1437
01:08:41,319 --> 01:08:43,024
covered in class or from others.

1438
01:08:43,024 --> 01:08:44,649
And I think one of
the wonderful things

1439
01:08:44,649 --> 01:08:46,450
is that you guys are
very well equipped now

1440
01:08:46,450 --> 01:08:48,367
to go out and start
answering these questions,

1441
01:08:48,367 --> 01:08:52,609
or other ones that you
think are important.

1442
01:08:52,609 --> 01:08:54,821
All right, I'll just close
with two more slides.

1443
01:08:54,821 --> 01:08:56,779
One is that if you like
reinforcement learning,

1444
01:08:56,779 --> 01:08:58,149
there is a lot of
people at Stanford

1445
01:08:58,149 --> 01:08:59,774
who think about
reinforcement learning.

1446
01:08:59,774 --> 01:09:01,100
There are lots of classes.

1447
01:09:01,100 --> 01:09:03,710
There's at least another five.

1448
01:09:03,710 --> 01:09:05,870
So there's Deep RL with Chelsea.

1449
01:09:05,870 --> 01:09:08,120
There's Decision Making
under Uncertainty with Mykel.

1450
01:09:08,120 --> 01:09:10,180
Mykel and I both
offer advanced courses

1451
01:09:10,180 --> 01:09:12,160
in needed decision making or RL.

1452
01:09:12,160 --> 01:09:15,609
And Ben Ryan Roy often
also offers an advanced RL,

1453
01:09:15,609 --> 01:09:16,790
or bandit class.

1454
01:09:16,790 --> 01:09:19,180
So there's lots of
places to learn more.

1455
01:09:19,180 --> 01:09:21,380
And finally, thanks for
being part of the course.

1456
01:09:21,380 --> 01:09:22,840
It's great to get
to meet everyone.

1457
01:09:22,840 --> 01:09:25,257
And we're really excited to
see your posters on Wednesday.

1458
01:09:25,257 --> 01:09:26,730
Thanks.

